<PlainLayout title="Gateway — Pilot Protocol (plain)" description="Bridge IP traffic to the Pilot Protocol overlay network - use curl, browsers, and any TCP tool." canonical="https://pilotprotocol.network/plain/docs/gateway/">

<p><a href="/plain/docs/">&larr; Docs index</a></p>

<h1>Gateway</h1>

<p>The gateway allows connecting to a service on a remote pilot node using standard TCP clients like `curl` or a web browser. The local connection port must match the remote service's listening port, as ports are not translated.</p>

<h2>Installation</h2>
<p>`pilot-gateway` is a separate binary not included in the standard install. It must be built from the `pilot-protocol/gateway` repository. The `pilotctl extras gateway` command resolves the binary's location by checking for it alongside `pilotctl`, in the system `$PATH`, or via the `$PILOT_GATEWAY_BIN` environment variable.</p>

<h2>How it works</h2>
<p>The gateway connects to a TCP service on a remote pilot node using standard TCP clients.</p>
<p>When started with a pilot address, the gateway performs the following actions:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Picks a local IP from a private subnet (default `10.4.0.0/16`) and adds it as a loopback alias on the network interface.</li>
  <li>Starts TCP listeners on that IP for every specified port.</li>
  <li>Tunnels incoming connections through the encrypted pilot overlay to the remote machine.</li>
</ul>
<p>The incoming connection on the remote node arrives at the same port number. The gateway does not translate ports.</p>
<p>`sudo` is required. Adding the loopback alias requires root privileges on macOS and Linux.</p>

<h2>Access a remote server</h2>
<p>To access a server running on a peer machine, for example a web server on port 80:</p>
<pre><code># 1. Trust the peer first (required)
pilotctl handshake agent-alpha

# 2. Start the gateway - maps 0:0000.0000.037D to 10.4.0.1
sudo pilotctl extras gateway start --ports 80 0:0000.0000.037D

# 3. Connect using any TCP tool
curl http://10.4.0.1/
# or open http://10.4.0.1/ in a browser

# 4. Stop when done
sudo pilotctl extras gateway stop</code></pre>
<p>The first mapped pilot address is assigned `10.4.0.1`, the second `10.4.0.2`, and so on.</p>
<p>To connect to multiple peers at once:</p>
<pre><code>sudo pilotctl extras gateway start --ports 80,8080 0:0000.0000.037D 0:0000.0000.0002
# First peer  → http://10.4.0.1/  and  http://10.4.0.1:8080/
# Second peer → http://10.4.0.2/  and  http://10.4.0.2:8080/</code></pre>

<h2>Expose a local server</h2>
<p>To expose a local server, run the service and have the peer connect using their gateway. No gateway setup is needed on the server side.</p>
<p>On the server machine:</p>
<pre><code># Start your server on whatever port you want
python3 -m http.server 8080
# nginx, caddy, your app - anything that listens on a TCP port

# Find your pilot address to share with the peer
pilotctl info
# Address: 0:0000.0000.xxxx  ← share this</code></pre>
<p>Approve the peer's handshake request:</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl pending            # see incoming requests
pilotctl approve &lt;node_id&gt;</code></pre>
<p>On the peer's (client) machine:</p>
<pre><code># --ports 8080 must match the port your server is actually on
pilotctl handshake 0:0000.0000.xxxx
sudo pilotctl extras gateway start --ports 8080 0:0000.0000.xxxx
curl http://10.4.0.1:8080/</code></pre>
<p>The pilot overlay handles traversal without requiring port forwarding, VPNs, or firewall changes.</p>

<h2>Manage mappings</h2>
<p>List current mappings:</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl extras gateway list</code></pre>
<p>Add a mapping to a running gateway:</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl extras gateway map 0:0000.0000.0007           # auto-assign local IP
pilotctl extras gateway map 0:0000.0000.0007 10.4.0.5  # assign a specific IP</code></pre>
<p>Remove a mapping:</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl extras gateway unmap 10.4.0.1</code></pre>
<p>Stop the gateway:</p>
<pre><code>sudo pilotctl extras gateway stop</code></pre>

<h2>Notes and limits</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Ports are not translated. The port specified in `--ports` is the port the remote machine must be listening on.</li>
  <li>`sudo` is required. Loopback alias creation requires root on macOS and Linux, regardless of port number.</li>
  <li>The gateway supports TCP only, not UDP.</li>
  <li>Mutual trust is required. Run `pilotctl handshake` first.</li>
  <li>If `--ports` is omitted, the gateway listens on default ports: `7, 80, 443, 1000, 1001, 1002, 8080, 8443`.</li>
  <li>Loopback aliases are removed automatically on `gateway stop` or `gateway unmap`.</li>
</ul>

